  "affiliation_unit": null,
  "affiliation_detail": "APT12 is believed to be linked to the Chinese People's Liberation Army (PLA). FireEye/Mandiant stated that APT12's targets are \"consistent with larger People's Republic of China (PRC) goals\" and that campaigns are \"in-line with PRC goals and self-interest in Taiwan.\" The NYT breach investigation found that computers used to launch the attack were believed to be the same university computers used by the Chinese military to attack U.S. military contractors.\nHowever, no DOJ indictment or formal government attribution to a specific PLA unit has been published. The PLA link remains an industry assessment based on targeting patterns, operational timing alignment with PRC strategic interests, and infrastructure overlap with known PLA-associated entities. No specific PLA unit designation has been publicly identified for APT12.\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "suspected",
  "entity_name": null,
  "last_seen": "2016",
  "first_seen": "2009",
  "first_described": "2012",
  "description": "APT12 is a Chinese cyber espionage group believed to have links to the PLA, active since at least 2009. \nThe group primarily targets organizations in East Asia — especially Taiwan and Japan — as well as media outlets, and entities whose interests intersect with PRC strategic concerns. \nAPT12 is best known for its 2012 breach of the New York Times, launched in apparent retaliation for the newspaper's investigation into the wealth accumulated by the family of then-Premier Wen Jiabao.\nThe group's defining operational characteristic is rapid adaptation to public disclosure. Mandiant dubbed APT12 \"Darwin's Favorite APT Group\" after observing the group retool its entire malware infrastructure at least twice in direct response to security vendor publications — first after the NYT breach exposure (2013), and again after Arbor Networks' detailed analysis of the Etumbot/RIPTIDE backdoor (2014). In both cases, APT12 paused operations only briefly before resuming with modified tooling specifically engineered to evade the detection methods described in the published research. \nBased on the reports consulted, the group's spear-phishing lure documents are typically written in Traditional Chinese and pertain to Taiwanese government interests.\n",
  "attack_group_id": "G0005",
  "attack_group_url": "https://attack.mitre.org/groups/G0005/",
